    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • OMODA 4 Lands in Paris To Showcase Futuristic Styling, Looks Like a Discount Urus\nJLR North America Recalls Nearly 94,000 Range Rover Vehicles Over Failing Rearview Cameras\n9 photos\nPhoto: Land Rover\nJaguar Land Rover's North American subsidiary has identified a potential issue affecting 93,997 units of the Range Rover. Affected 2022 through 2026 models were produced in Solihull for the US between July 8, 2021, and June 4, 2026. Solihull is the flagship manufacturing facility of the company, and Solihull also served as the home of the Land Rover Series and the body-on-frame Defender.\nThe drain holes for the rearview cameras in the subject Range Rover sport utility vehicles aren't enough to let water and moisture escape. If water pools where it shouldn't, the electric and electronic bits of the rearview camera slowly die, and that's not okay by National Highway Traffic Safety Administration standards.\nFederal Motor Vehicle Safety Standard 111 covers all things rear visibility. While it may not explicitly guarantee that rearview camera parts will never break down over the span of the vehicle, FMVSS 111 does outlaw backup cameras from not displaying the rearview image when reverse is selected. Such failures and even the occasional intermittent glitch mean the vehicle legally fails compliance.\nJLR North America estimates that 100 percent of the recall population carries the defect, conceding that five model years of the US-market Range Rover need replacement cameras. The tailgate's water management system is described in the recall documentation as potentially insufficient, allowing water to pool around the rearview camera installation. This water generates a few headaches, naturally.\nIn the first instance, moisture or water causes a distorted image. The worst-case scenario is no rearview image at all, increasing the risk of a crash or an injury. Care to guess how many claims and field reports JLR North America has logged so far? Make that 2,295 such claims and reports in the United States from August 3, 2022, through September 28, 2026.\nPhoto: Land Rover The safety boffins are not aware of any accident or injury reports. Jaguar Land Rover 's Product Safety and Compliance Committee opened an investigation back on July 31, 2026, prompted by anomalous rearview camera replacement rates on the current-gen Range Rover. Global warranty data had flagged an elevated replacement rate in Canada and the United States market, a rate no other market matched.\nThis, in turn, meant that a dedicated team was tasked with manually reviewing every claim for clues. The review ran through September 2026. Removed camera assemblies were shipped to the supplier for teardown analysis. Back in August, a separate camera recall affecting the Discovery line spurred the decision forum to ask for harder field evidence that the replacements were genuine failures rather than warranty noise.\nOn September 18, the committee was informed that a new process requiring evidence before approval of camera replacements had produced a significant decrease in claim rate. What that says about the pre-September 2026 claims is a question the recall filing published by the NHTSA unfortunately does not answer. The supplier ultimately told JLR that returned cameras had failed from water ingress.\nThis is the second time since 2024 the Range Rover has faced this problem. A separate campaign from two years ago covered 11,961 units of the fifth-gen model, specifically Range Rovers built from March 2022 to December 2023. That said, Jaguar Land Rover corrected the water ingress problem in production with the introduction of a revised seal for the tailgate trim piece housing the camera.\nDealers will be notified later in October 2026 with instructions to drill additional drain holes in the underside of the tailgate trim containing the camera. The functionality of the camera will be verified. If required, a replacement is on the menu. Interim customer notifications are due on December 11, 2026, but JLR North America has yet to finalize the planned remedy owner notification date.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nland rover Range Rover Land Rover Range Rover recall US safety\nDownload: Range Rover water-damaged rearview camera recall (PDF)\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT Tesla Roadster Uses New 4680 Cells That Promise To Fix Everything That's Wrong With the Cybertruck",
